B.Hd={Christmas:0,Halloween:1,Fall:2,Patriotic:3,NewYear:4,Cute:5,Cultural:6,Egyptian:7,French:8,Luxe:9,Movie:10,School:11,Streetwear:12,Sporty:13,Vintage:14,Enchanted:15}
B.d9=s(["Cats can hear ultrasonic sounds that both humans and dogs completely miss.","A cat's field of vision spans 200 degrees \u2014 they see the whole party.","Cats can smell 14 times better than humans. They found the treats already.",'When cats bring you "gifts," they\'re trying to teach you to hunt. How thoughtful.',"Cats sleep in sunbeams to regulate their body temperature. Solar-powered.","A cat's purr is also a self-soothing mechanism \u2014 they purr when stressed or healing.","Cats have 230 bones \u2014 24 more than humans. Built for every adventure."],t.s)
B.Dp=s(["Cats were brought to Europe by the Romans to protect grain stores.",'In Japan, the "maneki-neko" beckoning cat is a symbol of good fortune.',"Vikings kept cats on longships for rodent control and companionship.","In Turkish folklore, cats are said to guard mosques from evil spirits.","In Scotland, a black cat appearing on your doorstep is a sign of prosperity.","The Brothers Grimm's \"Town Musicians of Bremen\" \u2014 a donkey, dog, cat, and rooster \u2014 now stand cast in bronze, stacked on top of each other, in Bremen's town square."],t.s)
B.DH=s(["In ancient Egypt, cats were sacred. Harming one was punishable by death.",'The ancient Egyptian word for cat was "miu" \u2014 literally the sound they make.',"Archaeologists have unearthed over 300,000 mummified cats at Bubastis, the ancient Egyptian city sacred to the cat goddess Bastet.","The goddess Bastet was often shown holding a sistrum \u2014 a sacred rattle believed to bring health and prosperity."],t.s)
B.Et=s(['In 1842, Balzac published "The Heartaches of an English Cat" \u2014 a satirical fable about a vain cat chasing high society.','The iconic "Le Chat Noir" poster \u2014 advertising a 19th-century Parisian cabaret \u2014 remains one of the most reproduced images in art history.',"Legend says the Chartreux, France's velvety blue-grey shorthair, was once bred by monks for its calm temperament."],t.s)
B.EG=s(["The Ashera, the world's rarest cat breed, costs up to $125,000.","Cats sleep 12\u201316 hours a day. Living the luxe life comes naturally.","The richest cat in history, Blackie, inherited \xa37 million from his owner.","A cat's nose print is as unique as a human fingerprint \u2014 truly one of a kind.","Karl Lagerfeld's cat Choupette earned \u20ac3 million modelling in a single year.","Cats groom themselves up to 50% of their waking hours. True beauty regimen.","Sphynx cats cost upward of $3,000 \u2014 and they know exactly how rare they are."],t.s)
B.E4=s(["Jonesy the cat survived the entire Alien (1979) film. True main character energy.",'The cat in early James Bond films was never named in the script. Just "the cat."',"Mr. Bigglesworth in Austin Powers was played by a Sphynx named Ted NudeGent.","Orangey the cat won the PATSY Award (the animal Oscar equivalent) twice.","The cat in The Godfather was a stray Marlon Brando found wandering the set.","Cats have appeared in over 1,000 films. Hollywood's original stars.","The internet was practically invented for cat content. Studies suggest it.","A rare 1872 piano score for \"Alice in Wonderland\" put the Cheshire Cat's grin right on the cover \u2014 cementing its status as literature's most mysteriously helpful guide."],t.s)
B.EW=s(["Cats can recognise their name but often choose to ignore you. Academic.","A cat's long-term memory is 200 times better than a dog's.","Cats can solve simple puzzles \u2014 they just prefer to watch you struggle first.","Studies show cat owners score higher on intelligence tests. Draw your own conclusions.","Cats communicate with each other almost entirely through body language, not sound.","A cat's brain structure is 90% similar to a human brain. Uncanny.","Cats can learn to open doors, turn on faucets, and flush toilets. They choose not to."],t.s)
B.Ey=s(["Cats always land on their feet thanks to their built-in righting reflex.","A cat's whiskers are precisely as wide as its body \u2014 a built-in tape measure.","Cats can rotate each ear 180\xb0 independently. Maximum situational awareness.","A cat's claws retract completely \u2014 always sharp, always ready, never dull.","Cats walk with both paws on the same side simultaneously \u2014 a gait shared only with camels and giraffes.","A cat's tail contains up to 23 vertebrae \u2014 nearly 10% of all its bones.","Cats have a third eyelid called the nictitating membrane. Built-in sunglasses."],t.s)
B.Ed=s(["Cats can sprint up to 30 mph \u2014 faster than Usain Bolt.","A cat's jump can cover up to 6 times its body length in a single bound.","Cats can leap up to 5 times their own height from a standing start.","A cat's heart beats up to 140 bpm at rest \u2014 nearly twice a human's.","Cats have 32 muscles in each ear, giving them extraordinary directional hearing.","Cats can balance on surfaces less than an inch wide. Natural gymnasts.","A cat's flexible spine has 30 vertebrae \u2014 5 more than a human's \u2014 giving it extraordinary range of motion."],t.s)
B.Ef=s(["Cats were first domesticated over 9,000 years ago in the Near East.","Ancient Romans believed cats brought good luck and kept them as house pets.","The oldest known pet cat was buried alongside its owner in Cyprus, 9,500 years ago.","The first cat show was held at Crystal Palace, London, in 1871.","In medieval Europe, cat theft was considered a serious crime.","Abraham Lincoln kept four cats in the White House. The tradition endures.","Cats accompanied sailors on voyages for centuries \u2014 first mates of the sea.","An 1830s ad for Warren's Blacking Warehouse \u2014 where a young Charles Dickens once worked \u2014 starred a cat so dazzled by the shoe polish's shine it mistook its own reflection for a rival.",'Mark Twain, who kept dozens of cats, once joked that crossing a man with a cat "would improve the man, but deteriorate the cat."',"When Charles Dickens's cat Bob died in 1862, Dickens had one of Bob's paws made into an ivory letter knife \u2014 and kept it on his desk for the rest of his life.","Hemingway's six-toed cat Snowball started a dynasty \u2014 around 40 to 50 polydactyl cats still roam his old Key West home today, nearly all her descendants.","Victorian nonsense poet Edward Lear's most loyal companion through it all? His cat, Foss."],t.s)
B.Ex=s(["In Celtic mythology, cats guarded the gates to the Otherworld.","Cats can see in near-darkness using just 1/6 the light that humans need.","In Norse mythology, the goddess Freyja rode a chariot pulled by two giant cats.",'The Scottish "Cait Sith" was a fairy cat said to steal souls before they reached the afterlife.',"Ancient sailors believed cats aboard ships could predict storms before they arrived.","Cats were long thought to have magical powers in European folklore.","Black cats have been symbols of both extreme bad luck and great fortune \u2014 depending on the century.",'In Edward Lear\'s 1871 poem, the Owl and the Pussycat sail off with "plenty of money, wrapped up in a five-pound note" and a guitar for moonlit love songs.','In the Grimm fairy tale "Jorinda and Jorindel," the villain is a witch disguised as a black cat, snatching girls and turning them into caged nightingales.'],t.s)
